@* HelpAttestationPage: help topic "Signed attestation" at "/help/signed-attestation". Static text and a screenshot. *@
@page "/help/signed-attestation"

<HelpTopicFrame Slug="signed-attestation">
    <section class="card">
        <ul>
            <li>When every slot of a review is signed for its current state (the review is <strong>Approved</strong>),
                the review page offers <strong>Download attestation (PDF)</strong>. Until then the button is not shown.</li>
            <li>The PDF is created when you ask for it; ReviewCenter does not store it. It lists the project, the review,
                the repository revisions and the content fingerprint, every file with its status, the checklist with
                each answer and comment, the comment threads, who signed each reviewer slot and when, and the audit trail
                anchor at that moment. A project admin can leave some of this out (the discussion threads, the names,
                the checklist wording, the file list, the git context); every PDF then has a <strong>Content
                scope</strong> section that says what was omitted. See
                <a href="/help/evidence-settings">Evidence settings</a>.</li>
            <li>The PDF carries a <strong>digital signature</strong> that PDF readers such as Adobe Acrobat show and
                verify. The signature certifies the document with no changes allowed, so the reader reports any change
                to the file after it was signed.</li>
            <li>Each download is recorded in the audit trail.</li>
        </ul>
        <figure class="help-figure">
            <img class="help-shot" src="/help/signed-attestation-1.png" loading="lazy"
                 alt="An approved review: the Download attestation (PDF) button on its own line below the title, with a note about the signing key." />
            <figcaption>The download button of an approved review.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h2>What the signature proves</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>It proves that the document has not changed since it was signed, and that it was produced by the holder of
                the signing key named in the document (the certificate subject and thumbprint are printed in it).</li>
            <li>It does <strong>not</strong> prove who the reviewers are. ReviewCenter authenticated the reviewers when
                they signed, and recorded their sign-offs; the PDF lists those sign-offs, with the record digest each
                one bound to. The PDF vouches for them only as far as you trust the signing key.</li>
            <li>The signing time is the server clock. It is not a trusted timestamp.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>The signing key</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>By default ReviewCenter creates a <strong>self-signed</strong> key on first use and keeps it beside its
                Data Protection keys. The PDF and the review page say so: <em>SELF-SIGNED KEY - NOT AN ORGANIZATION
                KEY</em>. Use it for development and test only; PDF readers will say the signer is not trusted.</li>
            <li>For real use an administrator points ReviewCenter at an organization certificate, either in a certificate
                store (<code>Attestation:Provider</code> set to <code>CertificateStore</code>, with the certificate's
                thumbprint) or in a file (<code>Attestation:Provider</code> set to <code>File</code>, with
                <code>Attestation:CertificatePath</code>: a PFX file, or a PEM certificate with its key in the same file
                or in <code>Attestation:PrivateKeyPath</code>; a password comes from
                <code>Attestation:CertificatePassword</code> or <code>Attestation:CertificatePasswordFile</code>). The
                private key of a store certificate stays in the store, smart card or hardware module and is never
                exported; the key of a file is held in memory only and the file is never changed. Use a
                document-signing certificate (key usage digital signature or non-repudiation).</li>
            <li>The server warns about a certificate that ends within <code>Attestation:ExpiryWarningDays</code> (30 by
                default; 0 turns the warning off) or has already ended: the warning is in the log and in the key status
                that the self-test shows. Replace the certificate before it ends, because a PDF reader reports a
                signature of an expired certificate as not trusted.</li>
            <li>A certificate that is for code signing only is used, not refused for that reason: the PDF carries a note that Adobe
                Acrobat will not trust it for document signatures, and the server logs a warning at start-up. The server
                does not check the certificate chain or revocation; the PDF reader does.</li>
            <li>An administrator can set <code>Attestation:RequireTrustedKey</code> to refuse unsuitable keys. A
                self-signed key (the generated one, or a self-signed certificate from the store or a file) and a certificate
                whose key usage does not allow digital signature or non-repudiation are refused when the server starts:
                the server does not start, and the reason is shown in the startup error and the log. A certificate that
                stops being valid while the server is running (for example, it expires) is caught later: the review
                page then shows why attestation is unavailable instead of the button.</li>
            <li>If an administrator supplies a self-signed certificate (from a store or a file), the review page says so
                (<em>self-signed certificate supplied by the administrator</em>, not an organization key); the note
                says <em>generated by this server</em> only for the key that ReviewCenter creates itself.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</HelpTopicFrame>
